k security at the canadian  forces base in kandahar he says his  father and brother were killed by the  taliban while another brother was  recently found with photos of the  canadian military on his phone he says  the taliban took him away and beat him  nearly to death other videos shared with  global news appear to show taliban going  door to door conducting torture and  executions inside homes or in outlying  areas far from the capitol we cannot  trust them  because they are not trustable this  former canadian interpreter believes  that once the taliban achieves some  international concessions it will show  its true face whatever they say it's  just they also learned politics somehow  but  everybody knows the truth they fought  for 20 years so do you really think it's  going to be over like  never happen we are their enemies they  will take their revenge in 20 years he  says afghanistan has changed but the  taliban has not  jeff semple global news kabul  still ahead why it's taking longer and  costing more to build new houses  the pandemic has sparked a frenzy in  home buying if it's a new bill don't  expect to move in quickly the canadian  home builders association says the  average home construction project is  facing a delay of seven weeks and it'll  cost you more as ann gaviola explains it  all comes down to supply  maya and val's move into their first  house was delayed several times during  the pandemic they were supposed to take  possession in may but only got the keys  half a year later after the birth of  their first child  we were definitely  going through through some stress  lots of emotions at play but i guess we  were kind of able to get through it they  were lucky to be able to stay with  parents while they waited for their home  to be ready if you're going to be moving  make sure to  do it before  having the birth of a child i'll say  that kind of complicates things a little  bit the canadian homebuilders  association says this type of lengthy  hold up is becoming common from coast to  coast to coast on average  in canada we're seeing delays on  closings of about seven weeks about a  quarter of our members are experiencing  delays of more than nine weeks supply  chain snarls hit construction throughout  the home building process resulting in  lengthy delays now once people get into  their home there are long waits for  appliances plumbing materials windows  bathtubs even garage doors to slow  things down even more there's a shortage  of skilled laborers to tackle the  massive backlog the cost of raw goods is  a factor too the price of lumber hit  record highs earlier this year the  housing frenzy created an historic  lumber shortage tim hadl's home framing  business is busier than ever but he  can't hire enough people to meet all the  demand  it's a great problem to have but it is  frustrating he's been in the business  for two decades and says he's never seen  supply chain issues like he's seeing now  we've had to delay some projects just  because of  shortages of materials my crystal ball  says about another year before we are  actually back to a normal these extra  complications come as demand for houses  and more space has spiked because of the  pandemic putting additional strain on  limited supply and further stoking an  already hot housing market ann gaviola  global news toronto  up next nothing about us without us  youth calls for climate action  outer space just got spicier nasa  scientists have picked the first peppers  grown on the international space station  their hatch chili's a type found in new  mexico to celebrate the harvest one  astronaut used the peppers to make what  she calls her best space tacos yet  back on earth world leaders have a lot  on their plates during cop 26 in glasgow  the u.n climate summit is a chance for  leaders to push each other to move  faster on climate action crystal  commansing reports on the young people  fighting for a seat at negotiating  tables  young people are raising up their voices  and advocating for a safe clean livable  planet but being taken seriously is  another story we need to stop thinking  about old debates because we need a new  vision for a new future so you need to  invest your time money and effort in us  to shape our future  more than one million young people have  added their voices by signing a petition  online by the global citizen movement  advance unicef is also calling on  leaders to include young voices in the  decision-making process you know there  are young people who are doing very  brave direct action to show the world  and show their leaders why action needs  to be taken on climate change there are  young people who know every sub-clause  of the paris agreement who are in these  negotiation spaces who are advocating on  behalf of young people this is shakti  ram kumar's second cop summit a  sustainability undergrad at ubc she's a  leader with student energy an initiative  aimed at speeding up the transition to  renewables and her passion is shared  according to a survey of g20 nations  canadians have the strongest feelings  that were already in a climate emergency  both among adults and under eight teams  in canada it's it's really it's really  inspiring you are seeing young people  really say  we want climate action and we are not  going to stand by until we until we  really see it we've contributed  disproportionately to the climate crisis  we're one of the handful of wealthy  nations who have kind of taken more than  our fair share of the earth's resources  more than our fair share of the  emissions in the atmosphere and that  means now we can take accountability and  show some leadership a youth-led report  will be released thursday focusing on  the energy transition and where they  most feel investments should be made  young people from 120 countries  including canada help to craft it the  hope is that if young people are armed  with hard data it will add credibility  to their ideas  and